<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Akbar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1556">1556</a>–<a href="/years/1605">1605</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Akbar was the third Mughal emperor, reigning from 1556 to 1605. He expanded the empire across most of northern and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and built an efficient administration based on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansabdari-system">mansabdari</a> system and a reformed land revenue. His policy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/religious-tolerance">religious tolerance</a> and alliances with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> rulers made him one of the most important figures in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/akbar.jpg" alt="Akbar" width="311" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Govardhan / Mir Ali Heravi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Govardhan._Akbar_With_Lion_and_Calf_ca._1630,_Metmuseum_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Karrani Afghans, who took Gaur in 1564 and were led from about 1566 by Sulaiman Khan Karrani, ruled from Gaur and later Tanda, conquered Orissa in 1568 and outwardly acknowledged <b>Akbar</b> to avoid war.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bengal-sultanate">Bengal Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Only under <b>Akbar</b> (r. 1556–1605) did administrative consolidation, military expansion and a policy of religious accommodation transform a fragile conquest state into a stable and expansive empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Bahadur Shah of Gujarat sacked Chittor in 1535, and in 1567–1568 <b>Akbar</b> besieged and took the fortress, which was defended by Jaimal and Patta while Udai Singh II withdrew to the hills.</blockquote><a href="/empire/udaipur-state">Udaipur State · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…ministers were few after the early medieval period, but Jain merchants and scholars were often close to power, serving as advisers and financiers to Hindu and Muslim rulers; the Mughal emperor <b>Akbar</b> received Jain monks at his court and restricted the killing of animals during their holy days.</blockquote><a href="/religion/jainism">Jainism · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>In India, Persian was the language of government of the Delhi Sultanate and above all of the Mughal empire, whose emperor <b>Akbar</b> made it the language of all government records in 1582.</blockquote><a href="/language/persian">Persian · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>…Kawi Mahabharata of the late 10th century, in Kannada by Pampa and Kumaravyasa, in the Telugu Andhra Mahabharatam of the 11th to 14th centuries, in Bengali, Odia, Assamese, Marathi, Punjabi and Hindi, and in Persian, first for Sultan Zayn al-Abidin and then in the Razmnameh commissioned by <b>Akbar</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/mahabharata">Mahabharata · Reception</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Umarkot Fort, in what is now <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatehpur-sikri">Fatehpur Sikri</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=25.36,69.73&amp;year=1580" aria-label="Umarkot Fort on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6397 1231 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Akbar"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6847 1501h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6847 1501h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=25.36,69.73&amp;year=1580">What was here in 1580? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I have become convinced that the dark clouds of conceit and the mist of self-opinion have gathered round you, and that not a step can be made in advance without the torch of proof.&#8221;</p><footer>To the learned men of his court, Ibādat-khāna debate &#183; English translation quoted by G. B. Malleson, 1890 &#183; Abul Fazl's account in the Akbarnama, quoted in G. B. Malleson's Akbar (1890) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/31572/pg31572-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;That course only can be beneficial which we select with clear judgment.&#8221;</p><footer>To the learned men of his court, Ibādat-khāna debate &#183; English translation quoted by G. B. Malleson, 1890 &#183; Abul Fazl's account in the Akbarnama, quoted in G. B. Malleson's Akbar (1890) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/31572/pg31572-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Between me and Azíz is a river of milk, which I cannot cross.&#8221;</p><footer>Of his foster-brother Mirza Aziz Koka &#183; English translation quoted by G. B. Malleson, 1890 &#183; said while giving him the lightest of punishments &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/31572/pg31572-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I cannot bear that any misunderstanding should subsist between you and those who have for so many years shared in my toils, and been the companions of my glory.&#8221;</p><footer>On his deathbed, 1605 &#183; English translation quoted by G. B. Malleson, 1890 &#183; asking that his nobles be summoned to him &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/31572/pg31572-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Man's outward profession and the mere letter of Muhammadanism, without a heartfelt conviction, can avail nothing.&#8221;</p><footer>To the learned men of his court, Ibādat-khāna debate &#183; English translation quoted by G. B. Malleson, 1890 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/31572/pg31572-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
